// Pixel art as data: one character per pixel, '.' is clear, every other
// character is a palette key. Authored with a scratch generator, then
// committed as plain grids: edit the rows here directly.
//
// Chapter 2-1, the airport terminal at night. Same layout as the rooftop
// set so the chunk baker works unchanged: tiles are 16 x 16, anchor top
// left, each kind one anim whose frames are variants (fps 0, pick by column
// hash). roofTop is the polished terrazzo floor with its steel fascia,
// roofEdgeLeft and roofEdgeRight its ends, roofFill the dark structure under
// it (variant 1 a service hatch with a status LED, variant 2 a return air
// grille), oneWay a mezzanine ledge with a glass fascia, stone a clad
// column block, spikes a torn luggage belt (hazard), brick a taped box
// (breakable), auraBlock the sigma crate in terminal steel.
//
// walkway: 16 x 16, 4 frames per direction. The treads move 1 px a frame
// (period 4), the lit chevrons on the skirt 2 px a frame (period 8), so any
// run of walkway tiles drawn with the same frame scrolls seamlessly. left is
// right mirrored. Drive the frame from world time.
//
// Decor anchor is the bottom left (y is the top of the floor row it stands
// on). gateDoor (the goal): 4 frames of the BOARDING board's flaps
// settling, hold the last frame once settled. gateSign: the breakable sign,
// frame 0 on, frame 1 flicker (like neonSign).
//
// AIRPORT_BACKDROP: three 512 px tileable strips for the parallax, bottom
// on the horizon: far (the airfield, control tower, runway light rows), mid
// (the apron: parked planes, jet bridge, tug and cart train), near (the
// terminal's glass wall from inside: mullions, sill, rain beads; the glass
// is clear so the layers behind show through).
import type { Grid, Palette, SpriteSet } from './types';
import type { RooftopTile } from './rooftop';

